! OP and AD: the two indicators the model writes and nothing looked at.
!
! WHY THIS EXISTS.  audit_indicator_coverage.py asks which indicators
! the model WRITES that no program ever NAMES, and after three rounds of
! correcting the audit itself the answer was exactly two: OP and AD.
! Both are reachable, both are claimed in bsv/README.md, and neither
! half of either claim was checked anywhere.
!
!   OP, bit 15, "Operation Code Invalid".  README: instructions that are
!   decoded but not executed "set indicator 15, OP, and no-op, matching
!   real-machine behavior for a suppressed instruction".  24 setOpInvalid
!   sites in StretchExecute and nothing ever read the bit.
!
!   AD, bit 16, "Address Invalid".  Reference Manual p.20: an effective
!   word address beyond the installed core sets AD and the instruction
!   "is executed as a NO OPERATION".  The model's core is six units,
!   InstalledWords = 98304 = (8)300000 words, so (8)400000.0 is off the
!   end of it.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED.  Hand-written from the manual and from the
! README's own claim, not transcribed -- no deck block found so far
! reaches either bit.  What it pins is a documented model behaviour, so
! a regression that silently executed a suppressed instruction, or
! quietly honoured an address off the end of core, would be caught.
!
! ONLY ONE OF AD's TWO TRIGGERS IS HERE.  The other -- SIC storing
! below 19-bit address 16.0 -- is in ad_sic_indicator.strap, and it has
! to be a separate program: AD is SET-ONLY (nothing in the model clears
! it, and BB cannot, since p.41 only lets BB change indicator positions
! 20-63 and AD is bit 16), so a second AD case appended here would find
! AD already on and prove nothing.
!
! BOTH POLARITIES, which is the lesson from the index indicators.  A
! test that only checked the bits ON would pass a model that set them
! always, so each one is checked OFF after an ordinary instruction
! FIRST, and only then ON after the offending one.  OP and AD are
! set-only in the model -- nothing ever clears them -- so the off-check
! has to come first to mean anything, and it does.

        SLC, (8)2300.0
!
! ---- neither bit is on after an ordinary instruction ---------------
START   LX, $X1, WSEED               ! a perfectly ordinary index load
        BB, $OP, $                   ! hang up if OP on
        BB, $AD, $                   ! hang up if AD on
        L(N), FKONE                  ! an ordinary FP load, address installed
        BB, $OP, $                   ! hang up if OP on
        BB, $AD, $                   ! hang up if AD on
!
! ---- OP: a decoded-but-unexecuted operation ------------------------
! LVE is out of scope in this model (StretchExecute's dixop default),
! so it must set OP and change nothing.
        LVE, $X1, WOTHER             ! out of scope -> OP, and a no-op
        BZB, $OP, $                  ! hang up if OP off
        BB, $AD, $                   ! hang up if AD on -- OP must not imply AD
! and the suppressed instruction must not have touched the index:
        KV, $X1, WSEED               ! $X1 still equals the seed?
        BZXE, $                      ! hang up if not equal
!
! ---- AD: an effective address beyond the installed core ------------
! The accumulator still holds FKONE.  A floating-point load from off
! the end of core must set AD and leave it alone.
        L(N), (8)400000.0            ! beyond InstalledWords -> AD, no-op
        BZB, $AD, $                  ! hang up if AD off
        K(N), FKONE                  ! accumulator unchanged?
        BZB, $AE, $                  ! hang up if not equal
        B, PASS
!
WSEED   XW, 1, 1, 0, 0
WOTHER  XW, -1, 0, 0, 4              ! 4 = the flag, IBM bit 25
FKONE   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0400000000000000000000
PASS    BEW, $
        END, START
